{
  "diagnosis": "Failures cluster into three mechanisms: (1) indication mismatch — selecting an option that treats a comorbidity or symptom rather than the stated primary condition; (2) safety/contraindication neglect — ignoring stated comorbidities, current medicines, or procedure history that make an otherwise plausible option unsafe; (3) route/formulation and interaction reasoning — failing to prefer the safer route or to check the chosen drug against the patient's existing regimen. The agent also over-trusts the prior answer and under-uses explicit patient facts.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "primary_indication_match",
      "text": "Before choosing, restate the single stated diagnosis or problem the encounter is managing. Eliminate any option whose primary labeled use is a different condition, even if it treats a symptom or comorbidity mentioned by the patient. Prefer the option whose main indication directly matches the stated condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "stated_fact_safety_gate",
      "text": "Extract every explicitly stated patient fact: current medicines, comorbidities, prior surgeries/procedures, and relevant history. For each candidate option, ask whether any stated fact is a contraindication, precaution, or interaction. If a stated fact makes an option unsafe or requires monitoring, deprioritize it unless no safer indicated option exists. Never assume an unstated fact is present or absent."
    },
    {
      "id": "route_and_formulation_preference",
      "text": "When two options share an indication, prefer the route or formulation with lower systemic exposure or better local targeting if the patient has a condition that systemic exposure could worsen. Do not default to the systemic version when a topical, local, or otherwise lower-risk formulation is available and indicated."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_check_with_current_regimen",
      "text": "For every candidate option, check it against the patient's current medicines for known interactions or additive effects. If an option interacts with a current drug, either avoid it or explicitly note the required monitoring; do not select it silently. If no interaction is stated, do not invent one."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_symptom_only_traps",
      "text": "Distinguish treating the underlying stated condition from treating a secondary symptom. Options that only address a symptom (e.g., bleeding, mood, pain) without treating the stated diagnosis should be ranked below options that treat the diagnosis, unless the symptom is the stated problem."
    },
    {
      "id": "evidence_over_prior_answer",
      "text": "Base the choice on the stated condition and stated patient facts, not on the prior answer or on benchmark position. If the prior answer conflicts with the stated indication or a stated safety fact, follow the stated evidence and explain the conflict."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "stated diagnosis",
    "current medicines",
    "comorbidity",
    "contraindication",
    "interaction",
    "route of administration",
    "primary indication",
    "symptom vs condition"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated condition, current medicines, comorbidities, prior procedures, and relevant patient facts. Separate the primary diagnosis from secondary symptoms. Do not infer unstated facts.",
    "safety_check": "Check each candidate against stated contraindications, comorbidities, current medicines, and procedure history. Flag interactions and required monitoring. Do not invent facts or assume absent facts are present.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option whose primary indication matches the stated condition and whose safety profile is compatible with all stated patient facts. Prefer lower-risk routes/formulations when indication is equal. Do not use prior-answer or position shortcuts."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weights the most salient diagnosis or the most familiar drug class, then selects an option that is either not indicated for the stated condition or is contraindicated/less safe given current medicines. Failures cluster into: (1) indication mismatch where a plausible but wrong drug is chosen; (2) safety mismatch where a drug is indicated but interacts with or is worsened by current therapy; (3) failure to prefer the option that treats the active problem rather than a comorbidity or symptom; (4) failure to use the patient's current medication list to rule out options.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "Before comparing options, restate the active diagnosis or syndrome from the dialogue. Eliminate any option whose primary indication does not treat that active problem. Only then compare remaining options on safety and patient-specific factors."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_safety",
      "text": "When the patient reports current medicines, check each candidate option for clinically meaningful interaction or additive risk with those medicines. If an option is indicated but has a significant interaction or contraindication with a current medicine, prefer an equally indicated alternative without that interaction."
    },
    {
      "id": "treat_active_not_history",
      "text": "Distinguish the active problem from past medical history and from comorbid conditions. Choose the option that treats the active problem; do not select a drug solely because it matches an old diagnosis, a risk factor, or a symptom that is not the target of therapy."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_symptom_only",
      "text": "Do not choose an option merely because it relieves a symptom (pain, insomnia, anxiety) unless that symptom is the primary indicated target. Prefer disease-modifying or definitive therapy for the stated condition when such an option exists."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_not_present",
      "text": "If a patient fact needed to justify an option is not stated in the dialogue, treat it as unknown rather than assumed. Do not select an option that requires an unstated fact for its indication or safety."
    },
    {
      "id": "class_and_route_check",
      "text": "Verify that the option belongs to the drug class appropriate for the diagnosis and that its typical route/setting matches the scenario. Eliminate options from unrelated classes even if they are familiar or commonly prescribed."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "active diagnosis",
    "current medicines",
    "contraindication",
    "drug interaction",
    "indicated therapy",
    "symptom relief",
    "unstated fact"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the active diagnosis or syndrome, the current medicine list, and only explicitly stated patient facts. Separate active problem from past history and comorbidities. Mark any fact not stated as unknown.",
    "safety_check": "For each candidate, check contraindications and interactions against the stated current medicines and conditions. Do not invent missing facts. Flag options that are indicated but unsafe due to interactions or additive risk.",
    "option_selection": "First eliminate options not indicated for the active problem. Then among indicated options, prefer the one with the best patient-specific safety profile and fewest interactions with current medicines. Do not choose symptom-only or history-only matches."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "Failures cluster into three mechanisms: (1) indication matching is too coarse, so the agent picks a drug that is broadly in the right class but not the best-supported agent for the specific presentation; (2) safety reasoning is asymmetric, either inventing contraindications from incidental history or ignoring real drug-disease/drug-drug interactions; (3) option selection is distracted by superficial name similarity or by the patient's current medication rather than by the target diagnosis.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_specificity",
      "text": "When choosing among options, first map the stated diagnosis to the most specific indicated mechanism or drug class. Prefer the option that directly treats the diagnosed condition over options that are only supportive, symptomatic, or used for a different condition. If two options are in the same class, prefer the one whose labeled indication or standard use best matches the specific presentation described."
    },
    {
      "id": "safety_asymmetry",
      "text": "Do not infer a contraindication from a patient fact unless the fact is explicitly linked to that contraindication. Conversely, do not ignore a stated current medicine or comorbidity when it plausibly interacts with an option. Check each option against the stated condition, current medicines, and relevant patient facts, and eliminate options only for concrete, stated reasons."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_medication_context",
      "text": "Treat the patient's current medicines as context for interaction and safety, not as the target of treatment. The correct option should address the diagnosed condition while remaining compatible with current medicines; do not select a drug merely because it is related to a current medicine or because it shares a class with one."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_surface_similarity",
      "text": "Do not choose an option because its name, class, or common use superficially resembles another option or the patient's current medicine. Compare options by their actual indication for the stated diagnosis and by their safety profile in this patient, not by lexical or category similarity."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_handling",
      "text": "If a patient fact needed to distinguish between options is not stated, do not assume it is present or absent. Choose the option that is best supported by the facts that are stated, and avoid eliminating an option solely because of an unstated fact."
    },
    {
      "id": "first_line_preference",
      "text": "When multiple options could be appropriate, prefer the option that is standard first-line or best supported for the diagnosed condition. Reserve non-first-line options for situations where the first-line option is contraindicated or unavailable based on stated facts."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "specific indication",
    "first-line",
    "stated contraindication",
    "current medicine interaction",
    "surface similarity",
    "missing fact"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated diagnosis, current medicines, and relevant patient facts. Distinguish facts that are explicitly stated from facts that are merely implied or absent.",
    "safety_check": "Check each option against stated contraindications, current medicines, and comorbidities. Do not invent contraindications from unstated facts, and do not ignore stated interactions.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that best matches the specific diagnosis and is safest given stated patient facts. Prefer first-line or best-supported options, and avoid choices based on superficial similarity or on the patient's current medication."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weights the patient's current medication as the target of therapy, selecting the drug the patient is already taking or a drug named in the dialogue instead of the drug indicated for the diagnosed condition. It also fails to distinguish between a diagnostic/imaging agent and a therapeutic drug, and between a drug's general indication and the specific indication in the case. Safety checking is not the main failure; the main failure is indication matching and avoiding distractor options that are already in use or are not indicated.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "When choosing a medication, first identify the diagnosed condition and the goal of therapy (curative, symptomatic, replacement, prophylactic). Select the option whose primary indication matches that condition and goal. Do not select an option merely because the patient is already taking it or because it was mentioned in the dialogue; current therapy is context, not automatically the answer."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_med_context",
      "text": "Treat the patient's current medicines as context for safety and interaction checks, not as the default answer. If the patient is already on a drug for the same indication, consider whether the question asks for an addition, an alternative, or a change; do not simply re-select the current drug unless the evidence explicitly supports continuing or optimizing it."
    },
    {
      "id": "diagnostic_vs_therapeutic",
      "text": "Distinguish diagnostic, imaging, or tracer agents from therapeutic drugs. A diagnostic agent mentioned in the history does not treat the diagnosed condition and should not be chosen as therapy. Choose the option that is a therapeutic agent for the diagnosed disease."
    },
    {
      "id": "specific_indication_match",
      "text": "Match the option to the specific clinical indication, not just the drug class. For example, a corticosteroid formulation intended for local or intra-articular use is not interchangeable with a systemic corticosteroid for a systemic disease. Prefer the option whose route, formulation, and approved use fit the diagnosed condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "exclude_unrelated_options",
      "text": "Eliminate options whose primary indication is unrelated to the diagnosed condition (e.g., antipsychotics, antihistamines, anticoagulants, bladder antispasmodics) even if they are familiar or appear in the patient's history. The correct option should have a plausible mechanism for the diagnosed condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "safety_with_current_meds",
      "text": "After indication matching, check the leading option against the patient's current medicines and comorbidities for contraindications or interactions. Do not invent missing facts; if a fact is absent, treat it as unknown rather than present. Use safety to confirm or reject an otherwise indicated option, not to override a clear indication mismatch."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "diagnosed condition",
    "goal of therapy",
    "current medication",
    "diagnostic agent",
    "therapeutic agent",
    "specific indication",
    "route and formulation",
    "contraindication",
    "interaction"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated diagnosis, the goal of therapy, current medicines, and relevant patient facts. Separate diagnostic or imaging agents from therapeutic drugs. Do not treat a missing fact as present.",
    "safety_check": "Check the leading option against current medicines and comorbidities for contraindications and interactions. Use only stated facts; do not invent missing ones. Safety confirms or rejects an indicated option but does not replace indication matching.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option whose primary indication, route, and formulation match the diagnosed condition and goal of therapy. Do not default to the patient's current medicine or to a drug merely mentioned in the dialogue. Exclude options with unrelated indications."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weights a plausible but non-decisive comorbidity or prior medication, and under-weights the option that is specifically indicated for the diagnosed condition. It also fails to distinguish 'no stated contraindication' from 'safe/indicated,' and sometimes treats a drug that treats a symptom as if it treats the disease. The fix is to make indication match the primary diagnosis first, then apply patient-specific safety only to the leading indicated options, and to avoid inventing contraindications from unstated facts.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "primary_indication_first",
      "text": "When choosing a medication, first identify the single best-supported diagnosis from the case. Rank options by whether they are standard disease-modifying or definitive therapy for that diagnosis, not by whether they relieve a symptom or comorbidity. Eliminate options that do not treat the primary diagnosis before considering secondary factors."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_effect_direction",
      "text": "For each stated comorbidity or concurrent medication, determine whether it makes a candidate drug more appropriate, less appropriate, or neutral. Do not let a comorbidity override a clearly indicated therapy unless the comorbidity creates a specific, stated contraindication or a well-known major safety concern. If the comorbidity only changes monitoring or preference, keep the indicated drug as the leading choice."
    },
    {
      "id": "stated_vs_assumed_safety",
      "text": "Treat only explicitly stated patient facts as present. A missing allergy, pregnancy, renal function, or interaction history is not evidence of safety or danger. When safety data are absent, prefer the option with the strongest indication and the fewest known absolute contraindications, and do not invent a contraindication to justify a non-indicated alternative."
    },
    {
      "id": "refractory_and_resistance_logic",
      "text": "If the patient is already on an appropriate first-line drug and symptoms persist, consider whether the case describes treatment failure, resistance, or a need for adjunctive therapy. In that situation, choose an agent that is indicated for the refractory or resistant form of the condition rather than repeating the same class or choosing an unrelated drug."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_vs_disease_treatment",
      "text": "Distinguish medications that treat the underlying disease from those that only relieve a symptom such as pain, anxiety, or insomnia. A symptom-relief drug is not a substitute for definitive therapy unless the question specifically asks for symptomatic management. When two options are both plausible, prefer the one that addresses the diagnosed disease process."
    },
    {
      "id": "option_elimination_by_class",
      "text": "Before final selection, classify each option by drug class and intended use. Eliminate options whose class is not used for the diagnosed condition, even if they are familiar or commonly prescribed. Then compare the remaining indicated options on patient-specific safety and guideline preference."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "primary diagnosis",
    "indicated therapy",
    "contraindication",
    "refractory",
    "resistance",
    "symptomatic treatment",
    "drug class",
    "stated patient facts"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated diagnosis or leading diagnosis, current medicines, prior treatment failures, and relevant comorbidities. Separate explicit facts from assumptions; do not infer missing allergies, labs, or interactions.",
    "safety_check": "Check only stated contraindications and interactions. If a safety fact is missing, do not treat it as either safe or unsafe. Apply safety concerns to the leading indicated options rather than using an unstated risk to eliminate the best-supported drug.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that is standard therapy for the primary diagnosis, then adjust for stated patient-specific safety. Prefer disease-modifying or definitive therapy over symptom-only relief. If first-line therapy has failed, prefer an agent indicated for refractory or resistant disease."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent fails by (1) selecting a drug that is already ineffective or contraindicated for the stated condition, (2) ignoring prior treatment failure or patient-specific safety signals, (3) choosing an option that does not match the diagnosed indication, and (4) treating a missing fact as present. These are mechanism-level errors in evidence extraction, safety checking, and option selection.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "extract_indication_and_prior_treatment",
      "text": "Before choosing, explicitly identify the stated diagnosis or syndrome and any prior or current treatments for it. If a prior treatment is documented as ineffective, partially effective, or not tolerated, do not select that same drug again unless the question specifically asks about re-trial or dose adjustment. Prefer an alternative indicated option."
    },
    {
      "id": "screen_for_contraindications_and_interactions",
      "text": "Check each candidate option against all stated patient facts: organ dysfunction, prior adverse reactions, current medicines, and comorbidities. If a fact is not stated, do not assume it is absent or present. Eliminate options with a documented contraindication, significant interaction, or mechanism that conflicts with the patient's condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "match_option_to_diagnosis",
      "text": "Select only options whose primary indication matches the working diagnosis. Reject options that treat unrelated conditions, are adjunctive when a definitive therapy is required, or have no plausible benefit for the stated problem. When two options are in the same class, prefer the one with the better safety profile for this patient's stated comorbidities."
    },
    {
      "id": "avoid_repeating_ineffective_or_contraindicated_drug",
      "text": "If the patient is already taking a drug and reports inadequate response, or if the drug is contraindicated by a stated patient factor, do not choose that same drug as the answer. Instead, choose a different indicated agent or a different management strategy that addresses the root cause."
    },
    {
      "id": "use_negative_findings_correctly",
      "text": "Treat explicitly negative findings as active exclusions. If the patient denies a symptom, allergy, or risk factor, do not use that absent fact to justify a contraindication or to prefer a drug. Conversely, do not invent a missing fact to support an option."
    },
    {
      "id": "prioritize_definitive_indication_over_symptomatic_treatment",
      "text": "When the diagnosis is established, choose the option that treats the underlying disease rather than one that only relieves symptoms or treats a comorbidity. If the diagnosis is uncertain, choose the option that is safest and most appropriate for the leading diagnosis while awaiting confirmation."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "prior treatment failure",
    "contraindication",
    "drug interaction",
    "indication match",
    "missing fact",
    "definitive therapy"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Identify the stated condition, current medicines, prior treatment responses, and relevant positive and negative patient facts. Do not infer missing facts.",
    "safety_check": "Check each candidate against stated contraindications, interactions, organ function, and prior adverse reactions. Treat absent facts as unknown, not as present or absent.",
    "option_selection": "Choose the option that best matches the working diagnosis and patient-specific safety. Avoid repeating ineffective or contraindicated drugs. Prefer definitive therapy over symptomatic or unrelated treatment when the diagnosis is established."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "Failures cluster into four mechanisms: (1) selecting a drug that is already the patient's current therapy instead of an alternative/add-on; (2) ignoring a stated comorbidity or prior condition that changes drug safety (immune suppression, cardiac disease, bowel obstruction/constipation, prior syphilis); (3) ignoring stated concurrent medications that create interaction risk (warfarin, isotretinoin, beta-blocker); (4) choosing an agent whose indication does not match the diagnosed condition (eczema, angina, hyperkalemia, infectious disease). The agent also over-weights the first plausible option and under-uses the patient's own medication list and history as exclusion criteria.",
  "skills": [
    {
      "id": "current_medication_exclusion",
      "text": "Before selecting, list every medicine the patient is already taking. Any option that duplicates a current therapy is not the best choice unless the question explicitly asks about continuing, adjusting dose, or adherence. Prefer an alternative agent in the same or complementary class that is not already in use."
    },
    {
      "id": "indication_match_first",
      "text": "Match each option to the working diagnosis before considering secondary factors. Eliminate options whose primary indication is unrelated to the diagnosed condition (e.g., neuropathic, antihistaminic, oncologic, or acid-suppressing agents for an infectious, inflammatory, or electrolyte problem). The best option must plausibly treat the stated condition."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_safety_filter",
      "text": "Extract every stated comorbidity, prior condition, and organ dysfunction (immune suppression, cardiac disease, prior infection, bowel obstruction/constipation, gallbladder or pancreatic disease). Reject options that would worsen, contraindicate against, or fail to account for these facts. Do not assume a comorbidity is absent just because it is not restated."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_risk_ranking",
      "text": "When the patient is on interacting drugs (e.g., warfarin, isotretinoin, beta-blockers), compare options by interaction burden. Prefer the effective agent with the lowest risk of clinically significant interaction; if two options share a class warning, choose the one with the better safety profile for this patient's specific co-medications."
    },
    {
      "id": "missing_fact_handling",
      "text": "If a patient fact needed to choose safely is not stated, treat it as unknown rather than present. Do not invent allergies, labs, or history. Select the option that is safest under the stated facts and note the missing fact as a reason to avoid the riskier alternative."
    },
    {
      "id": "symptom_mechanism_link",
      "text": "Link the patient's presenting symptoms to the mechanism of the candidate drug. The chosen option should address the underlying mechanism (infection, inflammation, electrolyte excess, immune suppression) rather than only a downstream symptom, and should not aggravate the mechanism causing the presentation."
    }
  ],
  "focus_terms": [
    "current medication",
    "contraindication",
    "drug interaction",
    "indication match",
    "comorbidity",
    "immune suppression",
    "cardiac history",
    "bowel obstruction",
    "missing fact"
  ],
  "modules": {
    "patient_evidence": "Extract the stated diagnosis or syndrome, all current medicines with doses, prior conditions, and relevant negatives. Treat unstated facts as unknown, not absent. Flag any current drug that could itself be causing or worsening the presentation.",
    "safety_check": "Screen each candidate against stated comorbidities, current medicines, and organ function. Reject options that duplicate current therapy, worsen a stated condition, or carry a significant interaction with a listed co-medication. Do not assume safety from absence of a stated allergy or lab.",
    "option_selection": "Rank remaining options by indication match first, then by patient-specific safety and interaction burden. Choose the effective agent that is not already in use and that best fits the stated comorbidities and co-medications; if a needed fact is missing, prefer the safer option and state the uncertainty."
  }
}

{
  "diagnosis": "The agent over-weights the first plausible indication and under-weights patient-specific modifiers (current medicines, comorbidity, age, and prior therapy) that determine which otherwise-indicated option is safest or most appropriate. It also treats a named diagnosis as sufficient without checking whether the patient is already on the drug or whether a stated comorbidity changes the preferred agent.",
