    {
      "id": "indication_first",
      "text": "First identify the active condition from the stated symptoms and diagnosis. Only options that treat that condition are eligible; eliminate options whose primary indication is unrelated (e.g., cancer drugs, antibiotics, hormones, or symptom-only agents) before comparing within-class choices."
    },
    {
      "id": "current_medication_check",
      "text": "Before selecting, list every medicine the patient is currently taking. If an option is the same drug or same class already in use, treat it as continuation rather than a new choice unless the question asks about adjusting therapy. Prefer an alternative agent when the current drug is failing, causing side effects, or the scenario asks for a change."
    },
    {
      "id": "comorbidity_modifier",
      "text": "When a stated comorbidity could be worsened by a drug class (e.g., hypertension with sodium-retaining steroids, hypotension with vasodilators, arrhythmia with proarrhythmics), prefer the indicated option with the least adverse effect on that comorbidity. Do not assume a contraindication that is not stated, but do use the comorbidity to rank otherwise-indicated options."
    },
    {
      "id": "severity_and_route",
      "text": "Match the option to the severity and required route of the presentation. For severe or systemic disease, prefer systemic therapy over topical or long-acting depot forms; for mild or localized disease, prefer the least systemic option. Do not choose an injectable depot or unrelated formulation when an oral systemic agent is indicated."
    },
    {
      "id": "age_and_population",
      "text": "Adjust selection for the patient's age and population. In pediatric or deficiency-related presentations, prefer the agent approved for that age and deficiency state; avoid adult hormone or unrelated therapies. In older patients, prefer agents with fewer cardiac, renal, or interaction risks."
    },
    {
      "id": "interaction_and_contraindication",
      "text": "Check for interactions with current medicines and for stated contraindications. If two options are both indicated, choose the one without a documented interaction or contraindication with the patient's current regimen. Never invent a missing allergy, pregnancy, or lab value; if a fact is absent, do not treat it as present."
